/**

@page page_peano_performance_optimisation Performance optimisation

\tableofcontents

Performance work starts from a release build, the Grid Snapshot, and an
external profiler. Logging filters keep diagnostic output from dominating
the measurement.

# Interpreting the decomposition

@ref page_peano_runtime_analysis defines the reported fields. Three values
separate common scaling limits:

- `MPI Load Balance` measures geometric leaf balance across MPI ranks.
- `Thread Ceiling` estimates the limit imposed by the largest subtree
  task on each rank.
- The phase report line `Utilisation` measures how much of the sweep window the
  subtree tasks occupied across the available threads.

The first two are structural bounds. The third includes the effects of task
dependencies, rank-boundary waits, memory bandwidth, and the work that each
callback performs.

# Inter-rank imbalance

The adaptive owner table balances leaf counts at spawn-block or block-child
granularity. A localised refined region can still leave one indivisible child
heavier than the leaf count a rank targets. Application work can also be imbalanced
even when leaf counts are equal.

Useful responses are:

- Increase the regular base depth when coarse partition units limit the
  balance and the extra grid fits in memory.
- Increase the global problem size so each rank receives more useful cells.
- Reduce the MPI rank count when boundary and replicated-spine costs dominate
  a small problem.
- Use solver tasking or parallel kernels when leaf counts do not represent the
  true work.

Profiles and application counters determine whether expensive particles,
limiters, source terms, or local solver variants correlate with the heavy
rank.

# Intra-rank imbalance

Standing sweeps derive subtree tasks from spawn-level blocks and nested heavy
refined subtrees. Too few tasks, one dominant task, or a data container that
only one task may enter lowers the attainable utilisation.

- Increase the problem size or base depth when the tree exposes too few task
  roots.
- Inspect `Cells per Task` before attributing idle time to the scheduler.
- Use solver-created tasks or parallel kernels when a single cell callback
  dominates a balanced traversal task.
- Treat particle steps separately: particle containers make their rank
  traversal serial.

A high intra-rank ceiling with low thread utilisation calls for a profile of
the sweep. Ready-task starvation, interface dependencies, memory bandwidth,
and work outside the traversal tasks have different remedies.

# Boundary and replication overhead

Every rank stores the structural spine needed to reach its local cells. A face
or a vertex that two ranks hold also requires a local entity slot on each of
them and a frozen partner slot.
More ranks increase these costs and reduce the interior work available to hide
the non-blocking boundary exchange.

High `Spine Replication` together with good inter-rank balance indicates
overdecomposition. A larger problem or fewer MPI ranks increases the ratio of
owned work to structural and boundary data.

# Standing-sweep replay

The first standing traversal records its event streams. Later sweeps replay
flat per-task buffers. A profile dominated by callback code therefore points
to the application or solver kernels. A profile dominated by traversal merge,
reduction, or wait regions points to task interfaces or rank-boundary traffic.

# Adaptive mesh refinement

Refinement-plan evaluation performs integer lattice work proportional to the
request count. The substantial regrid costs are structure and topology
construction, transition derivation, pool migration, and the transition
sweep.

A criterion can ask only from cells that already exist, so refinement over
several additional levels requires several drains. Oscillating refine and
erase decisions multiply this cost. Stable thresholds and a suitable regular
base depth reduce repeated topology changes.

The dynamic-AMR report separates sweep count, wall time, rank balance, task
balance, and thread utilisation. Compare its before and after fields to see
whether the new mesh also changes the decomposition.

# Solver and kernel parallelism

The traversal task graph parallelises independent mesh regions. A solver can
also submit tasks through tarch::tasking or use parallel kernels inside one
callback. These forms complement the geometric decomposition when the mesh
does not provide enough independent subtree work.

Tasks must contain enough work to amortise scheduling and dependency costs.
Parallel kernels must preserve the callback's data ownership and complete
before the callback exposes results that another event consumes.

# MPI analysis

When adding ranks slows a run, compare leaves per rank, spine replication, and
the amount of interior work first. If those remain healthy, profile boundary
packing, MPI progress, and boundary-task waits. The exchange overlaps with
interior tasks only while such tasks remain ready.

Pinning can restrict the processors visible to a rank and therefore reduce the
thread count selected by tarch::tasking::Runtime. Confirm the runtime's thread
report before interpreting a hybrid scaling result.

*/
